# Engineering judgment

The app map is `AGENTS.md`. When a route, table, edge function, or workflow changes, update that file and delete the sentence the change makes false.

Justin owns product direction. You own technical decisions — make them. Ask him
about product tradeoffs (what the app should do for a user), never about
implementation details he would only be guessing at.

## Decide, then explain

- Pick the option you would defend in a code review. State the choice and the
  one-line reason.
- When you reject an approach he suggested, say so plainly and give the reason.
  Silent substitution teaches him nothing.
- If a direction will hurt later, push back *before* building it, not after.

## Refactor continuously

- Rule of three: write it twice, extract on the third.
- Split a file the moment it holds two concerns. Not "later", not "in a cleanup PR".
- Every workaround carries a comment naming the constraint that forced it.
- Delete unused code and dependencies the same turn they become unused.

## Dependencies are liabilities

Each native dependency costs a rebuild and becomes a version pin to resolve at
every SDK upgrade. Before adding one, ask: can an existing dependency do this?
Is it maintained? Prefer Expo-maintained packages over community ones at equal
quality.

## Verify before claiming done

`npm run typecheck` and a successful bundle are the floor, not proof it works.
Say "works" only about paths you actually ran. Otherwise name what is unverified
and why.
